Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to build a
Python command-line utility with the following specification: Build a standalone
Python 3 command-line script (intended to run under Termux on Android, with a
shebang pointing to the Termux Python interpreter) that cleans up a directory of
Python wheel files (.whl) by removing "empty" wheels and, optionally, wheels for
packages that are already installed. **Purpose and behavior:** 1. **Empty wheel
detection:** A wheel counts as empty if it contains no `.py` files and no other
non-directory content outside of `*.dist-info/`, `dist-info/`, and
`__pycache__/` entries.
Treat directory entries (names ending in `/`) as non-content.
Inspect wheels as ZIP archives using the standard library.
If a wheel cannot be read, print an error message including the path and the
exception, and treat it as not empty (do not delete it). 2. **Package
name/version extraction:** Derive the package name and version from the wheel
filename stem by splitting on hyphens: the first part is the name (with
underscores replaced by hyphens), the second is the version.
If there are fewer than two parts, return a pair of nulls. 3. **Installed
package detection:** Run the current interpreter (`sys.executable`) with `-m pip
list --format=freeze` via `subprocess.run` with captured text output and
`check=True`.
Parse each output line of the form `name==version` into a mapping of normalized
names to versions.
Handle failures gracefully (pip missing, non-zero exit) with a clear message and
a safe fallback (no installed packages assumed).
Normalize names for comparison consistently (lowercase, treat underscores and
hyphens equivalently). 4. **Main workflow (command-line interface using
argparse):** - Accept a directory argument (the directory to scan for `.whl`
files; default to the current directory if sensible) and flags controlling
behavior, such as a dry-run/preview mode that only reports what would be
removed, and an option to also remove wheels whose package is already installed
(matching by name, and optionally by version). - Scan the directory
non-recursively for `*.whl` files. - For each wheel, determine whether it is
empty and/or corresponds to an installed package, and decide whether to delete
it. - Delete with `pathlib`/`shutil`/`os`-style file removal; in dry-run mode,
delete nothing and only report. - Print a per-file status line and a final
summary with counts of scanned, removed (or would-be-removed), kept, and error
files. 5. **Error handling and edge cases:** - Non-existent or non-directory
target: print an error and exit with a non-zero status. - No wheel files found:
report that and exit cleanly with status 0. - Filenames that don't parse into
name and version: keep them and note it. - Deletion failures: catch the
exception, report it, count as an error, and continue with remaining files. -
Use `from __future__ import annotations` and type hints (`tuple[str, str] |
tuple[None, None]` style). - Exit codes: 0 on success, non-zero on fatal errors.
6. **Execution details:** Make the file executable with the Termux shebang
`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`.
Use only the Python standard library (argparse, shutil, subprocess, sys,
zipfile, pathlib, and similar).
Include a `main()` function guarded by `if __name__ == "__main__":` and return
an integer exit code from it passed to `sys.exit`.
Keep helper functions small, well named, and documented with brief docstrings or
comments where non-obvious.
Output the complete script as a single file with clear, readable formatting,
consistent with the style of the surrounding Termux-oriented tooling. ---
